n link back to other packages that  you wrote about um like there are two  really big financial packages that i've  explored quant mod and tidy quant and  then you can you know you just have more  options for content to talk about them  both and compare them  um  and then sometimes this is just like  my favorite one um  where  i was hungry and i  pulled up a picture of pasta because the  package that i was working with is  called data pasta it has nothing to do  with pasta it's just that i um  i  just wanted a picture of tortellini um  this this one was my favorite one  because  it definitely took a bunch of  packages to  um  start and so i  had a lot more steps in this one  but i felt that  it was a really good thing for people to  know because the whole thing is you can  cut and paste tables from any site you  want and then start playing with it in r  which is something i'd really wish that  i knew about in grad school um  so  i just want to  sum up and start summing up so that i  run out of time so i don't run out of  time um  i think the most important thing if you  want to start writing tutorials or if  even if you already do  you should think about the tutorials  that you liked when you were first  learning so for me  i really loved looking at database  tutorials on our bloggers because i'd  known about our bloggers for a long time  and i thought that it would be a really  good um  way to get started just because data vis  equals instant gratification you're able  to create something and if you're a  visual learner you're able to get a  result right away whether it's a chart  or whether it's one of the beautiful  visualizations that i looked at um  on our bloggers and it was just you know  you can you start with graphs and then  you start  really  kind of getting comfortable with the  syntax and that's what  i  really  that's what pushed me forward um  and then i also  was looking at w3schools for a variety  of different things um  it's not my favorite for r b